---
// Lesson 11.1a — Create PT Launch: Pick Your Program
// hybrid: CPT kickoff; requirements list; pick the program; spec it.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>11.1a — Create PT Launch: Pick Your Program</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The Create Performance Task is 30% of your AP score and it is
    built HERE, in B days, by you — due April 30 at 11:59 PM ET to
    the Digital Portfolio. Everything this year has been rehearsal:
    today the real thing launches with the decision that decides
    everything — what program.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity hybrid">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">CPT Launch</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The requirements, read from the horse's mouth.</strong>
    Your program MUST include, or the task scores zero on those
    rows:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>A list</strong> (or array/collection) used as a
            real part of the program</li>
        <li><strong>A procedure</strong> (your C# method) WITH at
            least one PARAMETER</li>
        <li><strong>An algorithm</strong> inside a procedure that
            uses <strong>sequencing, selection, AND iteration</strong></li>
        <li><strong>A call</strong> to that procedure, from
            somewhere that matters</li>
        <li><strong>Input, behavior, output:</strong> the program
            takes input, the code does something, something
            observable comes out</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Full details live on the
    <a href="../exam/create-performance-task.html">Create PT guide</a> —
    open it, read the Written Response prompts, and keep it open
    all unit. You have already built all five requirements at least
    three times: the card jam (<a href="10.2f.html">10.2f</a>)
    checked them off one by one.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The PT allows work you did in class this year. Compare
            two candidate strategies: port the card game (safe, all
            boxes ticked, familiar) vs. build the grid/symmetry jam
            (<a href="12.1a.html">12.1a</a> will do this later) or
            something new (riskier, fresher). Which produces a
            better WR2a answer ("how does your algorithm work")
            — the one you can EXPLAIN to death, or the one you
            can explain while half-asleep?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity hybrid">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Pick Your Program</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Three candidates, on paper, 5 minutes each:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>The card game port:</strong> shuffle + AI_Turn +
            hand lists. Requirements: pre-ticked. Risk: the
            graders' rubric rewards DIFFICULTY-APPROPRIATE
            algorithms, not difficulty-porn; yours is honest.</li>
        <li><strong>The platformer:</strong> your semester boss
            game — list of enemies, phase algorithm
            (<a href="8.2a.html">8.2a</a>). Risk: Unity projects
            are big for a PT video demo; a simplified build works
            fine.</li>
        <li><strong>The new idea:</strong> the thing you always
            wanted. Risk: unknown unknowns under deadline. If you
            pick this, its scope must fit 4 build days
            (<a href="11.1b.html">11.1b</a>–<a href="11.1e.html">11.1e</a>).</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>The decision ritual (write the answers in the
    repo):</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Which candidate can I explain at 11pm after a long
            day? (WR practice is graded on CLARITY)</li>
        <li>Which has the strongest "input → behavior → output"
            story a grader can SEE in a video?</li>
        <li>Which will still be interesting to me on build day 4?</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: PT red flags</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>A candidate "needs just one more system." Why are
                you FORBIDDEN to add systems after
                <a href="11.1e.html">11.1e</a> (the College Board
                records your process — and scope discipline is
                the skill being tested)?</li>
            <li>Your partner's candidate uses a list ONCE, in a
                dead end. Where does the rubric say the list must
                participate (meaningfully — in the actual
                behavior)?</li>
            <li>Your procedure candidate has zero parameters.
                Name the smallest change that fixes it (a
                parameterized difficulty, a threshold, a card —
                the <a href="10.2d.html">10.2d</a> score method
                takes one already).</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Tonight</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write CPT_PLAN.md in the repo: the candidate, the
                five requirements mapped to specific code pieces
                you will write, and the video's one-minute story
                arc.</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"11.1a: PT picked + plan"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Recite the five requirements and find them in candidate code</li>
            <li>Defend your program choice with the three questions answered</li>
            <li>Map each requirement to a specific method you will write</li>
            <li>State the deadline from memory: April 30, 11:59 PM ET</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../exam/create-performance-task.html">Create Performance Task</a> — requirements, prompts, rubric</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/index.html">Exam Hub</a> — every date and every guide</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="11.1b.html">11.1b — Create PT: Build Day 1</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="11.1a" title="11.1a Create PT Launch: Pick Your Program — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
